  "resources": {
    "costumbres": {
      "c-830f727233b6d0d2": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-652",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "See also Luke 8:44. According to the Mosaic law every Jew was obliged to wear a fringe or tassel at each of the four corners of the outer garment, one thread of each tassel to be deep blue. These tassels were to be to them a perpetual reminder of the law of God, and of their duty…"
      },
      "c-8b7cbd78081bac21": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-650",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "See also Mark 2:19; Luke 5:34. The “children of the bride chamber” were the friends and acquaintances who participated in the marriage festivities. The expression “child” or “children,” like that of “father” (see note on Gen. 4:20-21), is an Oriental form of speech, and is design…"
      },
      "c-e45cd75a9d23c8f2": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-649",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "See also Mark 2:9-12. The “bed” was simply a mat or blanket which could be carried in the hands. The poor sometimes had no other bed than the outer garment. See note on Deuteronomy 24:12-13 (#205). The wealthier people in the East have quilts or mattresses filled with cotton, whi…"
      },
      "c-e9d6566e597c26ce": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-651",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "See also Mark 2: 22; Luke 5:37. The use of bottles made from the skins of animals is very ancient, and is still practiced in the East. The skins of goats and kids are commonly taken for this purpose, and are usually so fashioned as to retain the figure of the animal. In preparing…"
      }
    },
